  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered rain showers continue across Northern\nMaine today, with a few snow showers possible early this \nmorning and late this evening, and another chance for snow \nshowers in the north Tuesday night\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nFor this morning, a weak surface trough remains across southern\nMaine through the Coastal Downeast region which could support\nsome light rain showers this morning, prior to further\ndissipating and drier conditions returning for Downeast through\nthe rest of the day today. In the north, a 700 mb vort max over\nthe St John Valley will provide enough lift that, combined with\nelevated moisture levels, will support continued rain showers\nthrough the day today. Early this morning, min temperatures\naround freezing could allow for a few of these showers to\ncontain snowflakes, but marginal enough that no measurable snow\naccumulation is expected. Temperatures lift into the 40s through\nthe day keeping precipitation rain. This evening, as\ntemperatures begin to drop to around freezing once more, any\nlingering showers could once again see some snowflakes mixing\nin, with no accumulation expected.\n\nA narrow ridge of high pressure allows for a brief pause in\nshowers for the CWA during the day on Tuesday, before the next\nshortwave late Tuesday will bring more showers into the area,\nparticularly across the north into Tuesday night. With\ntemperatures below freezing once more, some light snowfall is\npossible Tuesday night across the northern half of the forecast\narea. That said, there still remains some uncertainty with this\nsystem, depending on how quickly temperatures drop and how\nquickly this next low pressure system moves into the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for coastal storm development in the\nlater part of next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLarge amounts of uncertainty remain with the low pressure system\ndeveloping in the Mid-Atlantic and moving up the coast this\nweekend. Confidence has increased in a low pressure system\ndeveloping over the Ohio Valley, then intensifying as it moves E\ntowards the Mid-Atlantic. Confidence has also increased in\ncooler temperatures with the system from Friday throughout the\nweekend. Highest uncertainty remains in the track of the low and\nthe impacts that go with it. Any small variation in the track\ncould greatly change the impacts of the system.",
